  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "😇 Angel Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Real angel names with their meanings, or new ones in the same tradition: Raphael, \"God heals\"; Ithuel, Angel of Thresholds."
    },
    "intro": "Almost every angel's name ends the same way, and for a reason: <em>-el</em> is the Hebrew word for God, so Gabriel is \"God is my strength\" and Raphael \"God heals\". This **angel name generator** gives you the angels named in scripture and tradition, with their meanings, or new names built the same way, masculine or feminine. Add a domain, a choir or an epithet to give your angel a purpose.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Angels and their names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The word</strong> <em>angel</em> comes from the Greek <em>angelos</em>, a messenger, which translated the Hebrew <em>mal'akh</em>, meaning the same.</li><li><strong>The Bible names very few.</strong> Michael and Gabriel appear by name; Raphael is in the Book of Tobit, which is in the Catholic and Orthodox Bibles.</li><li><strong>The Book of Enoch</strong>, an older Jewish text, names seven archangels: Uriel, Raphael, Raguel, Michael, Sariel, Gabriel and Remiel.</li><li><strong>Later tradition</strong> added many more, from Raziel, keeper of God's secrets, to Azrael, the angel of death in Islamic tradition.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The nine choirs",
        "content": "<p>Around the year 500, a writer known as Pseudo-Dionysius arranged the angels into nine ranks, or choirs, which medieval writers took up and Dante built into his <em>Paradiso</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Seraphim</strong>, <strong>Cherubim</strong> and <strong>Thrones</strong>, closest to God.</li><li><strong>Dominions</strong>, <strong>Virtues</strong> and <strong>Powers</strong>, who govern the order of the world.</li><li><strong>Principalities</strong>, <strong>Archangels</strong> and <strong>Angels</strong>, closest to people.</li></ul><p>Choose \"With a choir\" to place your angel in one of them.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making a new angel name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Keep the ending.</strong> <em>-iel</em>, <em>-ael</em> and <em>-uel</em> make a name sound angelic at once. The new names here all use them.</li><li><strong>Give them one job.</strong> Traditional angels have a single task: healing, secrets, death. An Angel of Lost Things or of Thresholds is more memorable than an angel of everything.</li><li><strong>Fiction borrows freely.</strong> Castiel in <em>Supernatural</em> comes from Cassiel; Aziraphale in <em>Good Omens</em> is invented, but sounds right because of that ending.</li><li><strong>Female angels</strong> are rare in tradition, where the named angels are male, but common in fiction. The feminine names here keep the angelic sound with softer endings: <em>-aela</em>, <em>-ielle</em>, <em>-essa</em>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What does -el mean in angel names?",
        "answer": "\"God\", in Hebrew. Most angel names are short sentences about God: Michael is \"who is like God?\", Raphael \"God heals\", Uriel \"God is my light\"."
      },
      {
        "question": "Who are the seven archangels?",
        "answer": "The Book of Enoch lists Uriel, Raphael, Raguel, Michael, Sariel, Gabriel and Remiel. Other traditions give different lists of seven, often including Jophiel, Zadkiel or Chamuel."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names for a character?",
        "answer": "Yes. The new names are free to use and you don't need to credit the site, and the traditional names belong to everyone. For the other side, try the <a href=\"demon-name-generator.html\">Demon Name Generator</a>."
      }
    ]
  }
}
